cartridge "DELTA WAR/GAZETTE/1988"

' DELTA WAR
' A ColecoVision game based on "Delta War", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, January
' 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, DELTA WAR at $0801 started by
' SYS2061, loaded by DELTA WAR.BOOT). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/deltawar.dis, and run in jsc64: orig/c64time-deltaw.mjs),
' the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: two delta-wing fighters as
' smooth sprites in 16 headings, a framed starfield arena, a glowing star or
' a black hole in the centre, fireballs, bursts, sound effects.
'
' The game: two fighters duel until the clock runs out. Turn with the
' stick, push up to thrust, the button fires (three fireballs in flight at
' most), pull down to jump through hyperspace. A fighter hit by a fireball
' (anyone's, even its own), by the other fighter, or by the star or black
' hole, blows up; the other pilot scores one. Then a new round starts from
' the sides. Highest score when the time is up wins.
'
' From the original (orig/): the options (bounce off or pass through the
' boundaries; no gravity, black hole or star in the centre; short, medium
' or long fireballs; 1 to 9 minutes; hyperspace yes or no); the 16
' headings and their thrust table (-3,-5,-6,-7,... in 1/16 pixels a step,
' $1063/$1073); a speed accepted only while under 32 on each axis; a turn
' every second step; fire on the other steps, from three slots per pilot
' ($108D), fireball speed three times the thrust vector, launched from the
' nose ($10C7/$10D7 offsets); fireballs move twice per step; the step
' counter of 0-7 that times turns, fire, hyperspace (step 0 only) and
' gravity; gravity toward the centre with the thrust vector of the heading
' that points there, given at odd steps from the distance (halvings of the
' distance: thresholds 0,1,2,4,7 on the counter, $1096), only every other
' time for the star, speed under 33; hyperspace to a random place, keeping
' speed; the bounce reverses the speed; a fireball that hits anything is
' gone; ships that touch each other both blow up and nobody scores; the
' starting places (red at the right, upper half, facing left; blue at the
' left, lower half, facing right); the clock in minutes and seconds,
' stopped while a ship explodes.
'
' Liberties: the C64 ran about 0.8 steps per frame (measured in jsc64,
' 1.2-1.4 jiffies per step): here a step happens 4 frames in 5, motion is
' spread over every frame (positions in 1/64 pixels) and the arena is
' 224 x 160 instead of 296 x 160; collisions are distance tests (the C64
' used its sprite collision registers, which also killed a ship touching
' the black hole's invisible black characters: here the black hole is
' drawn); the C64's fireball range option had no effect (every fireball
' lasted 256 steps whatever was chosen, a slip in $0B4C): here SHORT is 96
' steps, MEDIUM 160 and LONG 256 (the C64's); far from the centre gravity
' acts once in 8 steps (the C64 read a stray byte there); a one-player game
' against a computer pilot (the C64 needed two players); fireballs of the
' same pilot pass through each other (the C64's could collide; this saves
' frame time); the explosion is three bursts at a calm pace (the C64
' cycled the colours every frame);
' the game end shows the winner, then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 flies the red ship, controller 2 the blue one
' (or the computer). Stick left/right turns, up thrusts, down jumps through
' hyperspace, either button fires. On the title: up/down choose a line,
' left/right (or the button) change it, the button on START plays.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29; tiles from code 128; the frame loop is
' protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. Own xorshift random numbers.

